MobileGlues: MG_BC_HALF=1 keeps the game's atlases at half their width and height, a quarter of the memory.

Prototype. Where BC is converted (Mali, PowerVR), a texture whose level 0 is 1024 wide or more is kept at half the
game's size, each block the average of the 2x2 the game gave under it (the sprites are premultiplied, so a plain
average). The game draws its sprites by where they are in their atlas as a fraction of it, so they're where it looks,
softer. An upload grows to whole pairs of blocks, its neighbours read from the copy every upload already has, and the
game is told its own sizes; read back, a level is the game's, at its size.
The shader decodes and encodes from one place each: the driver's compiler copies a function wherever it's called, and
the game waits for it as it starts (a second ASTC encoder took a Mali-G610 1.2 s and 300 MB more).
Shaders that ask a 2D texture's size (textureSize) or fetch its texels by position (texelFetch) were written for the
game's size: Factorio's trees turn a pixel position from textureSize back into a place in the atlas with the size it
knows, and with a halved atlas their leaves came from other sprites. Each such sampler gets a shift uniform, 1 while
the texture bound to it is kept at half, set before each draw: the size shifted up, a texel's position down.
